# kiosk-linux/configs/kernel-audio.fragment
# Audio hardware for the Panasonic Toughbook FZ-55 (all generations: Mk1/Mk2/Mk3
# all ship Intel HD Audio + a Realtek codec — confirmed by the Mk3 datasheet's
# "Integrated dual-array mic w/ AI noise reduction" + "92db speakers", and this
# is the standard codec pairing on every Intel U/P-series mobile platform from
# Whiskey Lake through Raptor Lake).
#
# Genuinely required, not a nice-to-have: RMPG Flex has a real audio subsystem
# (radio PTT/panic voice, voice alerts, console audio) that depends on this
# terminal actually having working sound. Before this fragment, ZERO audio
# kernel config existed anywhere in this project — the OS would boot with no
# sound device at all, silently.
#
# Every symbol below verified against the real pinned kernel 6.6.63 source
# (grep on sound/*/Kconfig), not guessed — SOUND and SND are `menuconfig` gates
# off by default in a minimal config, so they must be explicitly turned on or
# the entire audio subsystem compiles out regardless of the driver-specific
# options below.
CONFIG_SOUND=y
CONFIG_SND=y
# default y once SND is on, but stated explicitly rather than relied upon —
# defaults are exactly the kind of assumption this audit is meant to remove.
CONFIG_SND_PCI=y
CONFIG_SND_HDA_INTEL=y
# HDMI 4K output (Mk3 spec) carries audio over the same HDA controller; without
# this the display works but any audio routed to an external monitor is silent.
CONFIG_SND_HDA_CODEC_HDMI=y
# Realtek ALC-series is the codec on essentially every Intel-platform laptop of
# this era; GENERIC is a zero-cost fallback if the specific codec ID Panasonic
# used is not one of Realtek's own IDs.
CONFIG_SND_HDA_CODEC_REALTEK=y
CONFIG_SND_HDA_GENERIC=y
